# nightlight.schema — which layer may decide each key of the `nightlight` subsystem.
#
# Read by /usr/lib/w/w-conf-lib.sh (and its Python twin) during the load
# pass; a user-layer assignment to a `system` key is dropped with a warning, and
# `wconf_set` refuses to write it in the first place. See w-conf.md.
#
# Format: <KEY>\t<scope>       scope: system | user | both
#   system — root domain: the admin (or fleet policy) decides, per machine.
#   user   — the logged-in user may override it in ~/.config/w/nightlight.conf.
#   both   — either layer may speak; the usual priority order applies.
#
# Every key here is `user`. The night light lives entirely inside one graphical
# session — it colours that user's own screen, writes only that user's config,
# and restarting the daemon is `systemctl --user`. There is no root domain to
# protect, so there is no polkit anywhere in this subsystem. A fleet that does
# want to fix these (a shift-work deployment, say) still can: policy.d sits above
# the user layer and locks the setters, which is the mechanism for exactly this.

MODE	user
NIGHT_TEMP	user
DAY_TEMP	user
NIGHT_START	user
NIGHT_END	user
RAMP_MINUTES	user
